The '''War of the Four Sons''' was the succession war that destroyed [[Great Zhongyuan]] after the death of [[King Yuanxiong of Zhongyuan|King Yuanxiong]] in 300 CE. Yuanxiong died unexpectedly at the age of 54, and contemporaries widely suspected poisoning. His four sons had already been assigned to major administrative regions of the empire, and each had built a political base around the officials, garrisons, and court networks of his own province. When the succession crisis began, the imperial government split into competing regional regimes that fought for the right to inherit Great Zhongyuan.

The war lasted until 318 CE and ended with the fragmentation of Great Zhongyuan into the successor kingdoms of [[Haizhou]], [[Nanyang]], [[Jiaozhi]], and [[Zhongyuan]]. Later historians treated the conflict as the decisive moment in the collapse of the imperial order, because it transformed a centralised empire into four rival states.

== Background ==

In the final years of his reign, King Yuanxiong relied on a system of regional administration to govern the vast territory of Great Zhongyuan. His four sons were placed in separate command districts as a way of preparing them for rule and keeping the empire stable after his death. The arrangement was intended to bind the princes to the imperial system, but it also gave each of them an independent power base.

The princes were close in age, all born within a few years of one another, and they had grown up together in the same courtly environment before being sent to their respective regions. By the time Yuanxiong died, they were young men rather than mature monarchs, ranging from their early twenties to their late twenties. Their youth contributed to the instability of the succession, since each prince depended heavily on local advisers and military patrons.

== Death of King Yuanxiong ==

Yuanxiong's death came suddenly in 300 CE. Although the official court record did not establish the cause, rumours of poisoning spread quickly through the capital. The king's unexpected death at 54 deprived Great Zhongyuan of its most authoritative political figure at the exact moment when succession needed to be controlled from the center.

The court initially attempted to preserve continuity by recognising the senior imperial institutions, but the princes and their regional supporters moved too quickly for a peaceful settlement. Ministries, palace guards, and provincial officials split into competing camps, each claiming to defend the legitimate order. The succession crisis became a war almost immediately.

