The princes were not strangers to administration. Each had been expected to learn government through his assigned region, and each had developed patronage ties among the officials posted there. Hailin was strongest on the coast, where trade and naval support were concentrated. Nanyan controlled the inland routes that connected the grain-producing regions to the rest of the empire. Jiaozhe held the southern marches and the river approaches. Zhongwei, the youngest, retained the prestige of the capital and the ceremonial legitimacy of the dynasty.

== Course of the war ==

The conflict began as a contest for the capital and the right to issue imperial decrees, but it quickly expanded into a struggle over the provincial structure of Great Zhongyuan. Hailin presented himself as the protector of commercial order, Nanyan claimed to defend the grain supply, Jiaozhe positioned himself as the guardian of the southern frontier, and Zhongwei argued that the capital alone could confer legitimate succession.

At first, the imperial court tried to mediate among the brothers, but the negotiations failed as each prince consolidated the region under his control. Provincial governors, military commanders, and city magistrates were forced to choose between the competing administrations. In many areas, the war became less a campaign of set battles than a reordering of political loyalty.

Over time, the conflict hardened into four durable regional power centres. None of the princes was able to eliminate the others, and none could restore the old imperial unity without the cooperation of his brothers. The fighting therefore became a prolonged war of exhaustion, in which legitimacy, supply lines, and regional patronage mattered as much as battlefield success.

By the late 310s, the imperial army and treasury had been broken apart by the rival administrations. In 318 CE, the last serious efforts to restore a single imperial government collapsed, and the war ended in the effective dissolution of Great Zhongyuan.

== Outcome ==

The settlement that followed the war did not restore the old empire. Instead, each claimant's regional base crystallised into a successor kingdom. Prince Hailin's coastal domain became [[Haizhou]], Prince Nanyan's inland administration became [[Nanyang]], Prince Jiaozhe's southern territory became [[Jiaozhi]], and Prince Zhongwei retained the central region that later historians continued to call [[Zhongyuan]].

The fragmentation of Great Zhongyuan reshaped the peninsula's political geography. The successor kingdoms inherited the institutions, tax systems, and military structures of the imperial era, but they no longer recognised a single imperial centre. Great Zhongyuan survived only as a historical memory and as a claim to legitimacy used by later rulers.

== Legacy ==

The War of the Four Sons became one of the foundational political tragedies of Zong history. It demonstrated how regional administration could preserve an empire in the short term while creating the conditions for a succession crisis in the long term. Later historians often treated the war as the decisive warning that dynastic centralisation required both a clear inheritance system and effective control over provincial appointments.

In later memory, the conflict also became the bridge between Great Zhongyuan and the later fragmented era. The successor kingdoms that emerged from the war formed the political landscape that would eventually be transformed during the much later unification of Zong.

